0K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Fe2+ abundant\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CO reacts with water (H2O) via water-gas shift to form H2 and formaldehyde on magnetite surfaces.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well-established water-gas shift reactions form H2 and C1 compounds; demonstrated in hydrothermal vent simulations (Martin & Russell, 2007).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose condensation to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"calcium hydroxide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"calcium hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"alkaline pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde undergoes aldol condensation forming glycolaldehyde under basic surface pool conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formose reactions are one of the most robust, laboratory-demonstrated mechanisms for C2 carbonyl assembly (Weber, 2001).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxyamination of glycolaldehyde to aminoacetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 7-9\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycolaldehyde is aminated with ammonia to produce aminoacetaldehyde and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mineral-catalyzed amination is supported for aldehydes (Fitz, 2019); comparable steps have been shown under plausible prebiotic conditions.\"\n    },\n    